A 15-case differential regression also checks legacy creator/extractor versions,
ASCII UTF-8 flags and DOS timestamp fields at each of three member positions.
Every CRC-valid variant keeps its admitted payloads unchanged, cannot claim
fixed writer framing, and reframes into exactly the baseline stored-ZIP bytes.

Checked ZIP member names can now be projected into caller-bounded reference
slots without allocating a fresh vector or copying string bytes. Insufficient
space returns an exact required count without modifying a destination slot;
the full 65,534-member ZIP32 boundary retains source-pointer identity and the
extra output slots remain unchanged. This is inventory evidence, not Import.

A 1,024-case deterministic generated ZIP32 corpus now exercises varied member
subsets, Unicode names, binary lengths and opaque root payloads through both
byte writer and checked reader. Every emitted source must reproduce exactly
from its borrowed entries; a CRC-valid date mutation must clear the fixed
writer-framing claim without changing member contents.

Already-admitted typed manifests can now seal strictly ordered observed ZIP
names without allocating a separate inventory set. The sorted path rechecks
order and membership, returns typed failures, and shares existing Import
purpose, encoding and budget gates through opt-in read-only preflight.
The general manifest inventory API remains order-agnostic and unchanged.

The sorted profile Import path is also exercised with real CRC-checked ZIP
