The read-only typed `manifest.json` decoder now maps sealed ZIP-root JSON into
`ProfileManifest` under independent string, feature and entry limits. It
requires exact first-release fields, rejects unknown wire keys and ambiguous
numeric or SHA-256 spellings, and retains unknown optional feature identifiers.
The real stored-ZIP regression connects the decoded declarations through
sorted Import preflight and cryptographic member verification; a new CRC cannot
conceal a substituted payload. Canonical JSON writing, filesystem authority,
section decoding, migration and accepted-profile mutation remain open.

The new pure `atrament_profile_manifest_json_writer` serializes admitted typed
manifest metadata into deterministic UTF-8 under caller-owned entry, feature,
string and exact encoded-byte limits. Keys use fixed ASCII lexical order,
entries sort by exact archive path, feature lists sort by UTF-8 bytes, SHA-256
is lowercase hex, `u64` sizes are plain decimal, and strings use minimal
JSON-required escaping while preserving non-control Unicode. Both byte
planning and emission run through the same sink, with a single fallible output
reservation.

Six tests prove golden bytes, input-order invariance, control
escapes and max-integer round trips, exact limit refusal, typed-manifest
rejection, and a real stored ZIP whose source-attached decoded manifest passes
complete SHA-256 payload verification. This closes only the manifest JSON
writer prerequisite: there is no full profile Export adapter, physical file
authorization, section serializer, ZIP64 implementation, or session acceptance.

The canonical JSON writer additionally exposes exact-size planning and
caller-owned slice emission. Both reuse the allocating writer's typed admission,
limit checks, sorted ordering and byte emitter, including JSON string escaping.
A short slice reports the exact required count without mutating any slot; extra
capacity stays untouched.

A 512-case deterministic manifest corpus compares both byte-for-byte,
independently decodes the result and confirms a canonical round trip. This is
bounded in-memory serialization only; it creates neither a complete profile
artifact nor authorized filesystem Export.
